#!/bin/sh
# reference-transaction - the COMMIT-PATH PROXY (dark-factory-unification U5).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. U5 asks for "mechanical guards for worktree/cloud agents (hook-bypass
# detection at minimum; commit-path proxy where feasible)". Detection already existed:
# .githooks/pre-commit step 6 writes a tree attestation and
# scripts/checks/check-hook-attestation.ps1 reads it back. But detection is AFTER THE
# FACT - it runs when a reviewer looks. A bypassing agent's commit still lands on its
# branch, and whether anyone ever looks is a human question, which is exactly the class
# of assurance PLAN 0 A7 recorded as FALSIFIED.
#
# agent-org solved the equivalent problem with a PROXY: inside the worker container `git`
# on $PATH IS little-coder/git-proxy/git_proxy.py, so a push to main is not reported, it
# is refused (little-coder/git-proxy/git_proxy.py:154 _PROTECTED_BRANCHES). A worktree
# agent on this host has no such chokepoint - it runs as the operator's own user with the
# real git.exe on PATH, and any PATH shim it can be given it can also step around.
#
# THE ONE CHOKEPOINT THAT IS LEFT is inside git itself, and it is this hook. Measured, not
# assumed (2026-08-30):
#
#   git commit --no-verify   -> pre-commit SKIPPED, commit-msg SKIPPED,
#                               reference-transaction STILL RUNS.
#   exit non-zero at state=prepared
#                            -> "fatal: ref updates aborted by hook", exit 128,
#                               the branch does NOT move, the new commit is left dangling.
#
# So `--no-verify` - the documented, reached-for, trace-free bypass that motivated the
# attestation ledger - cannot advance a branch any more. It is stopped, not noticed.
#
# THE RULE. At `prepared`, for every refs/heads/* update, the (tree, message) pair of the
# commit the branch is about to point at must be in the attestation ledger. Anything else
# aborts the whole transaction and writes an audit record.
#
# THE MESSAGE JOINED THE PAIR ON 2026-08-30, and the tree alone was a real hole:
# attestation used to be written by pre-commit, which runs BEFORE commit-msg, so a message
# refused by commit-msg left its tree attested and the immediate `--no-verify` retry was
# waved through - unstopped AND unaudited. The attester moved to commit-msg, the last hook
# that can veto. Reproduction, measurement and the normalisation rule that keeps the two
# sides in agreement: .githooks/attest-lib.sh.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHECKED, because a guard that fires on honest work gets
# switched off and then protects nothing (the lesson check-hook-attestation.ps1 paid for):
#
#   - refs that are not refs/heads/* (refs/remotes, refs/tags, refs/stash, HEAD,
#     AUTO_MERGE, ORIG_HEAD). Nothing there is a commit path.
#   - ref DELETION (new value all-zero).
#   - a commit that is ALREADY REACHABLE from some existing ref. That is a ref MOVE, not
#     new content: `git branch`/`git switch -c`/`git worktree add -b` at an existing
#     commit, `git reset` in either direction, a multi-commit fast-forward, a pull whose
#     objects arrived under refs/remotes first. Measured shapes are in
#     scripts/agent-harness/verify-commit-path-guard.ps1 (the "ref move" cases). This is
#     reachability, computed from the repo - not a date, not an environment variable.
#   - anything committed while the SEQUENCER is running (rebase, cherry-pick, revert).
#     git does not run pre-commit for replayed commits, so their trees cannot be
#     attested and demanding it would block every reviewer rebase in MERGE-PROTOCOL.md.
#     These are LOGGED as SKIP-SEQUENCER rather than passed silently, and
#     check-hook-attestation.ps1 still reports them afterwards - the after-the-fact net
#     is not removed, it is what remains for this case.
#
# WHAT IT CANNOT STOP, stated plainly because a guard oversold is a guard mistrusted, and
# because a boundary asserted in prose is exactly how one gets oversold. Every route below
# is MEASURED by scripts/agent-harness/verify-commit-path-guard.ps1 (step 11), which also
# records, for each one, whether a guard-log line is written and whether the resulting
# commit is still caught after the fact by scripts/checks/check-hook-attestation.ps1 - the
# check queue.ps1 -Submit runs. The agent runs as the operator's own user with the real
# git.exe, so there is no privilege boundary here to hide a policy behind. The honest
# statement of this guard is "it closes the cheap route and prices the rest".
#
#   R1  git -c core.hooksPath=/nonexistent commit ...    LANDS. no guard line. UNATTESTED.
#       Turns off every hook. Also the operator's deliberate escape hatch.
#   R2  git update-ref refs/pre/x <sha>, then            LANDS. no guard line. UNATTESTED.
#       git update-ref refs/heads/b <sha>
#       Parking under a non-refs/heads ref makes the commit "already reachable", so the
#       second update is judged a ref move. Narrowing the reachability exemption would push
#       this to `git tag` and start refusing an honest `git checkout -b rel <tag>`, so the
#       exemption is kept wide deliberately. The DIRECT route is closed: update-ref straight
#       into refs/heads/* is refused.
#   R3  rm .githooks/reference-transaction, then         LANDS. no guard line. UNATTESTED.
#       git commit --no-verify
#       core.hooksPath is `.githooks` - a RELATIVE path INSIDE the worktree - so the guard
#       is a file the guarded party can delete. Two words, cheaper than R1.
#   R4  a worktree whose branch does not carry           LANDS. no guard line. UNATTESTED.
#       .githooks/reference-transaction
#       Same root cause as R3: one repo, one config, opposite outcomes per worktree. Every
#       branch cut before this hook merged is in this state until it merges the line.
#   R5  git rev-parse HEAD > "$(git rev-parse           LANDS. SKIP-SEQUENCER. UNATTESTED.
#       --git-dir)/CHERRY_PICK_HEAD", then
#       git commit --no-verify
#       Forges the sequencer state this hook exempts. The exemption is not optional: git
#       runs no pre-commit for replayed commits, so a rebase or cherry-pick can never be
#       attested, and demanding it would refuse every reviewer rebase in MERGE-PROTOCOL.md.
#       A forged .git file is indistinguishable from a real one to a hook that can only
#       read .git.
#   R6  git commit --amend --no-verify --no-edit         LANDS. no guard line. ATTESTED.
#       The one --no-verify shape that survives, and the reason it is allowed to: the tree
#       AND the message are unchanged, so the attestation genuinely covers this commit. It
#       can re-parent and re-date; it cannot alter what the commit says or what it holds.
#
# WHAT IS *NOT* A ROUTE, contrary to the obvious guess: deleting the ledger. This guard
# fails CLOSED - a missing ledger denies everything with reason=no-ledger. It is
# check-hook-attestation.ps1 that a deleted ledger neutralises (it reports INACTIVE), so the
# two mechanisms fail in OPPOSITE directions and that is worth keeping.
#
# R1-R5 all leave the commit UNATTESTED, and that is what check-hook-attestation.ps1 reads
# at the submit gate. So none of them is invisible: what they escape is the guard LOG, not
# the audit. What changed is cost and trace - `--no-verify` was one word that left no
# evidence at all; each surviving route is a deliberate, separately-visible command that
# still surfaces at the gate. Full table: documentation/notes/u5proxy-findings.md.
#
# Activate in a fresh clone with:  git config core.hooksPath .githooks
# Turn off deliberately (operator):  git -c core.hooksPath=/nonexistent <cmd>

[ "$1" = "prepared" ] || exit 0

# THE SHARED DEFINITION IS MANDATORY. Without attest-lib.sh this hook cannot compute the
# message digest the attester computed, so it refuses rather than falling back to the
# weaker tree-only rule - a guard that silently degrades to the check it replaced is
# precisely the failure this item exists to close.
_lib="$(dirname "$0")/attest-lib.sh"
if [ ! -r "$_lib" ]; then
    echo "COMMIT-PATH GUARD: .githooks/attest-lib.sh is missing - refusing to advance any branch." >&2
    exit 1
fi
. "$_lib"

common=$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$common" ] || exit 0
gitdir=$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)
[ -n "$gitdir" ] || gitdir="$common"
ledger="$common/hook-attest.log"
guardlog="$common/hook-guard.log"
now=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)

# The sequencer's state lives in the PER-WORKTREE git dir, not the shared one.
seq=""
for m in rebase-merge rebase-apply CHERRY_PICK_HEAD REVERT_HEAD; do
    [ -e "$gitdir/$m" ] && seq="$m"
done

denied=""
while read -r old new ref; do
    case "$ref" in refs/heads/*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
    # all-zero new value = deletion
    case "$new" in *[!0]*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
    git rev-parse -q --verify "$new^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || continue

    if [ -n "$seq" ]; then
        printf '%s SKIP-SEQUENCER ref=%s commit=%s state=%s\n' "$now" "$ref" "$new" "$seq" \
            >> "$guardlog" 2>/dev/null || true
        continue
    fi

    # Already-known history => this is a ref MOVE, not new content.
    if [ -n "$(git for-each-ref --contains="$new" --count=1 --format='%(refname)' 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
        continue
    fi

    tree=$(git rev-parse -q --verify "$new^{tree}" 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$tree" ] || continue
    # The digest of the message git actually STORED, reduced by the same function the
    # attester applied to the message FILE. One definition, in attest-lib.sh: if the two
    # sides ever disagreed, the guard would either deny honest commits or wave a rewrite
    # through, so they are not allowed to be two pieces of code.
    msg=$(_attest_digest_commit "$new")
    if [ -n "$msg" ] && [ -f "$ledger" ] && grep -q "^$tree $msg " "$ledger" 2>/dev/null; then
        continue
    fi

    reason="unattested"
    [ -n "$msg" ] || reason="no-digest"
    # A tree that IS attested but under a DIFFERENT message is the amend/retry shape. It is
    # worth naming separately: an operator reading this log should be able to tell a message
    # rewrite from unchecked content without re-deriving it.
    if [ "$reason" = "unattested" ] && [ -f "$ledger" ] && grep -q "^$tree " "$ledger" 2>/dev/null; then
        reason="message-not-attested"
    fi
    [ -f "$ledger" ] || reason="no-ledger"
    printf '%s DENY ref=%s commit=%s tree=%s msg=%s reason=%s wt=%s\n' \
        "$now" "$ref" "$new" "$tree" "${msg:--}" "$reason" "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" \
        >> "$guardlog" 2>/dev/null || true
    denied="$denied $ref"
done

[ -z "$denied" ] && exit 0

echo "" >&2
echo "COMMIT-PATH GUARD: refusing to advance$denied" >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "  The commit this branch would point at has content, or a message, that the hooks" >&2
echo "  never validated. The usual cause is 'git commit --no-verify', 'git merge" >&2
echo "  --no-verify', or a 'git commit --amend --no-verify' that rewrote the message." >&2
echo "  Nothing was lost: the commit object exists, the branch simply did not move." >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "  Re-run the commit WITHOUT --no-verify. The checks it skips are the secret guard," >&2
echo "  the line-ending rule, the LLM-gateway routing rule, the compose/ps1 structural" >&2
echo "  check and the env_file scope rule - each of which exists because it caught a real" >&2
echo "  failure. If a check is genuinely wrong, fix the check." >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "  IF AN HONEST COMMIT IS BEING REFUSED, check the hook set is CONSISTENT. The" >&2
echo "  attester is .githooks/commit-msg and it must come from the same commit as this" >&2
echo "  file: a half-merged .githooks/ - new guard, old attester that still writes the" >&2
echo "  ledger from pre-commit - denies everything, because the old attester records a" >&2
echo "  tree with no message digest and this hook requires the pair." >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "  Audit record: $guardlog" >&2
echo "" >&2
exit 1
